    "takeoff_ground_roll_provenance": {
      "title": "Takeoff",
      "conditions": "TAKEOFF PERFORMANCE. PAVED LEVEL DRY RUNWAY. GROSS WEIGHT 2325 LBS. NO WIND. FULL POWER BEFORE BRAKE RELEASE. EXTRAPOLATION OF CHART ABOVE 7000 FT. IS INVALID. Plotted against DENSITY ALTITUDE (FT.), 0 to 7000 ft, vs TAKEOFF DISTANCE (FT.). The sheet carries four curves keyed by a legend of FLAPS / ROTATION AIRSPEED / GROUND ROLL / AIRSPEED THROUGH 50 FT. / OVER 50 FT.: 25 deg flaps rotate 52 mph, through 50 ft at 66 mph (ground roll = the dotted curve); 25 deg flaps rotate 66 mph, through 50 ft at 66 mph (ground roll = the plain curve, over 50 ft = the open-circle curve); 0 deg flaps rotate 65 mph and 0 deg flaps rotate 72 mph, both through 50 ft at 72 mph (over 50 ft = the dashed curve). ALL AIRSPEEDS ARE MPH-CAS. ground_roll_ft here is the dotted curve (25 deg flaps, 52 mph rotation) and over_50ft_ft is the open-circle curve (25 deg flaps, 66 mph over the obstacle) -- the pair the handbook's own General Specifications page publishes as 1065 ft and 1760 ft.",
      "read_from": "chart",
      "figures": [
        {
          "field": "ground_roll_ft",
          "page": 122,
          "figure": "9-2",
          "title": "TAKEOFF PERFORMANCE"
        },
        {
          "field": "over_50ft_ft",
          "page": 122,
          "figure": "9-2",
          "title": "TAKEOFF PERFORMANCE"
        }
      ],
      "reference_point": {
        "field": "ground_roll_ft",
        "weight_lbs": 2325,
        "pressure_altitude": 0,
        "oat_c": 15,
        "value": 1065,
        "how": "The sheets print no worked example and no dashed walk-through path, so the anchor is the handbook's own GENERAL SPECIFICATIONS page 1-1 (PDF page 13), whose figures are stated to be 'for standard airplanes flown at gross weight under standard conditions at sea level' and 'subject to the same conditions as on the corresponding performance chart from which it is taken'. Standard sea level is density altitude 0, which is where I read each curve to check. Page 1-1 prints 'Takeoff Ground Run (minimum) (ft) 1065' and 'Takeoff Distance Over 50-ft Obstacle (25 deg flaps) (ft) 1760'. Read at density altitude 0 the dotted ground-roll curve gives 1080 ft (+15, +1.4%) and the open-circle over-50 curve gives 1750 ft (-10, -0.6%). The two anchors bracket the calibration in opposite directions, so no origin shift can satisfy both; the mesh calibration used here is unbiased across the pair (mean error +3 ft)."
      },
      "reference_point_error": 10.0,
      "unanchored": null,
      "read_tolerance": 25,
      "read_by": [
        "reader 1",
        "reader 2"
      ],
      "cells_agreed": 70,
      "cells_disagreed": 0,
      "basis": "poh_chart",
      "source": {
        "doc": "Cherokee Warrior Information Manual, Piper Model PA-28-151 (Handbook Part No. 761 563; Report VB-535, with the FAA Approved Airplane Flight Manual as Report VB-573). Applicability page: serial numbers 28-7415001 through 28-7615435.",
        "publisher": "Piper Aircraft Corporation",
        "note": "This handbook publishes performance as charts, not tables. The figures carried here were READ off its curves against a calibrated rule (scripts/poh/chart_rule.py) by two readers working independently, and are published only where the two agreed. They carry the basis \"poh_chart\" and are not as exact as a printed number."
      },
